    <slider x="15" step="0.01" y="185" max="0.5" width="240" display="kernel-width" height="50" min="0.01" direction="Horizontal" default="0.15" variable="kernel-width"></slider>
    <button x="140" y="245" height="45" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="115">go</button>
    <button x="15" y="245" height="45" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="115">setup</button>
    <slider x="15" step="0.1" y="300" max="20" width="240" display="time-scale" height="50" min="4" direction="Horizontal" default="10.0" variable="time-scale"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="0.001" y="355" max="1" width="240" display="growth-center" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.15" variable="growth-center"></slider>
    <slider x="15" step="0.001" y="410" max="0.1" width="240" display="growth-width" height="50" min="0.001" direction="Horizontal" default="0.015" variable="growth-width"></slider>
    <chooser x="15" y="465" height="60" variable="color-scheme" current="0" width="240" display="color-scheme">
      <choice type="string" value="Viridis"></choice>
      <choice type="string" value="Magma"></choice>
      <choice type="string" value="Monochrome"></choice>
    </chooser>
    <slider x="15" step="0.5" y="530" max="5" width="240" display="brush-size" height="50" min="1" direction="Horizontal" default="1.5" variable="brush-size"></slider>
    <button x="140" y="590" height="45" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="115">reset-states</button>
    <button x="15" y="590" height="45" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="115">draw</button>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[
## WHAT IS IT?

Lenia is a well-known form of artificial life created by Bert Chan. It consists of a system of continuous cellular automata: a grid of cells that repeatedly update from a simple local rule, building complex patterns from the bottom up. Unlike a discrete automaton such as Conway's Game of Life, where a cell is only alive or dead, each Lenia cell holds a value that varies smoothly from 0 (empty) to 1 (full) and changes in small steps. From random speckles the grid organizes into lifelike shapes that move, hold their form, and repair themselves.

Lenia is continuous in three aspects: its cell states are smooth values rather than simply on or off, its sense of space is smooth because each cell blends with a wide soft neighborhood, and its passage through time is smooth because the world advances in small increments. These make the world flow instead of flicker, and out of that flow emerge shapes that behave like simple organisms. Blobs, rotating rings, and gliding arcs form on their own, travel across the grid, recover after being disturbed, and can even be sorted into "species"; the best known is Orbium, a crescent that swims steadily along. For this reason Lenia is widely studied as a model of artificial life, a mathematical world in which lifelike organization emerges from simple rules.


## HOW IT WORKS

Each cell has a state, ranging from 0 (empty) to 1 (full). At each tick, each cell looks at its neighbors, judges whether it is in a good place to grow or a too-empty or too-crowded place to shrink, and adjusts its state slightly. The whole grid updates together, and repeating this is what brings the world to life. The rule has three parts, described below: a neighborhood weighting called the kernel, a growth response, and a small update step.

First, each cell looks at its surrounding neighbors, but these neighbors do not have equal influence. The influence of the neighbors are weighted with a bell curve, the smooth hump

$\text{bell}(x,\mu,\sigma) = \text{exp}\left(-\frac{\left((x - \mu) / \sigma\right)^2}{2} \right)$

which equals 1 when $x$ sits exactly at $\mu = \text{KERNEL-CENTER}$ and tapers smoothly toward $0$ as $x$ moves away, with $\sigma = \text{KERNEL-WIDTH}$ setting how quickly it fades. The kernel applies this hump to distance: a neighbor's weight depends on how far it lies from the cell, written as a fraction $r$ of the cell's reach, so that $r = 0$ at the cell itself and $r = 1$ at the farthest neighbors (KERNEL-RADIUS cells away). Placing the hump's peak at $r = \text{KERNEL-CENTER}$ ($0.5$ by default) with spread KERNEL-WIDTH makes neighbors about halfway out count for the most and the nearest and farthest count for almost nothing, giving the kernel the shape of a soft, blurry ring. Every weight is then scaled so that all of them add up to 1. This is akin to focusing objects at a medium distance, so objects at the focused distance are clear but objects that are near and far appear hazy. In this analogy, the clear objects or neighbors have higher influence and the hazy ones have less.

Second, each cell blends the weighted values of their neighbors into a single number: it multiplies each neighbor's state by that neighbor's weight and adds the results. Because the weights add up to 1, the total is a weighted average of the neighborhood, a value between 0 and 1 called the potential and written denoted by $U$. In plain terms, the potential $U$ answers the question, how full is my ring-shaped neighborhood right now? (This weighted sum over a neighborhood is the operation mathematicians call a convolution of the grid with the kernel.)

Third, the potential is converted into a value called growth. Each cell has an ideal neighborhood fullness, determined by the setting GROWTH-CENTER. If $U$ is close to the GROWTH-CENTER, the cell has just the right amount of life around it and should grow; if $U$ is well below or well above the GROWTH-CENTER, the cell should shrink. This growing or shrinking operation is performed by plugging the potential value $U$ into a growth function, which is another bell curve centered at GROWTH-CENTER with spread GROWTH-WIDTH, and then stretching that hump's output onto the range from -1 to +1. The growth function is given by,

$\text{growth}(U) = 2 \times \text{bell}(U, \text{GROWTH-CENTER}, \text{GROWTH-WIDTH}) - 1$

So when $U$ equals GROWTH-CENTER the growth is +1, the fastest possible growth; as $U$ moves away from GROWTH-CENTER the growth drops down to -1, the fastest possible decay. The spread GROWTH-WIDTH controls how forgiving this is: a small  GROWTH-WIDTH rewards only a narrow band of fullness, while a larger  GROWTH-WIDTH rewards a wider range of neighborhoods.

Finally, the cell changes its state by only a fraction of that growth, and the result is kept in range:

$\text{new state} = \text{clip}( \text{state} + (1 / T) * \text{growth}(U) )$

Here clip just pushes any value below 0 back up to 0 and any value above 1 back down to 1. The setting $T=\text{TIME-SCALE}$, controls the step size: dividing by $T$ means each step moves the state only a little, so a large $T$ gives many tiny, smooth updates (slow, fluid change) and a small $T$ gives fewer, larger jumps. This is a gentle repeated nudging, rather than a binary on-or-off switch. Which is what makes Lenia continuous in time and lets its patterns flow. It is worth noting that decreasing the TIME-SCALE too much can force the growth updates to be too large and cause all cells to die.

Lastly, the grid wraps around at all four edges, so a shape that drifts off one side comes back on the opposite side and never runs into a wall.


## HOW TO USE IT

Press SETUP to build a world, then GO to run it. To paint your own pattern, hold down the DRAW button and drag the mouse across the view; press RESET-STATES to clear the world to empty, which is handy before drawing from scratch.

The controls above the SETUP button shape the world and take effect only the next time you press SETUP. The INITIAL-CONFIG chooser picks the starting pattern: "Random", or one of the named creatures (Orbium, Gyrorbium, Scutium, or Discutium), each of which automatically loads the settings it needs to survive. The KERNEL-RADIUS sets how far out each cell looks, and KERNEL-CENTERr and KERNEL-WITDH set where its ring of influence peaks and how thick that ring is.

The controls below the SETUP button take effect immediately, even while the model is running. The TIME-SCALE sets how gradually the world changes, with higher values giving slower, smoother motion. The GROWTH-CENTER is the ideal amount of surrounding life a cell wants, and GROWTH-WIDTH is how forgiving it is about being off from that ideal. The color-scheme chooses the color palette, and BRUSH-SIZE sets how large the drawing brush is when the DRAW button is toggled.


## THINGS TO NOTICE

Start from random initial configurations and watch it organize itself. Within a few moments the noise condenses into smooth blobs, rings, and arcs instead of simply fading away.

Load a creature from the INITIAL-CONFIG chooser and notice that it keeps its shape while it moves. Orbium, Scutium, and Discutium travel steadily across the world, while Gyrorbium stays in one spot and spins.

Watch a gliding creature closely and you will see it rebuild itself as it travels: new material brightens along its leading edge while the trailing edge fades. It holds its shape not by staying still but by constantly renewing itself.


## THINGS TO TRY

While a creature is running, nudge growth-center or growth-width by a tiny amount. The balance is delicate: a healthy creature may suddenly dissolve into nothing or bloat into a solid, overgrown mass.

Change kernel-radius or kernel-width and press SETUP to rebuild the world. Even from the same kind of random start, a wider or narrower ring of influence grows completely different patterns.

Press RESET-STATES, hold the DRAW button, and paint a blob of your own. Does it fade away, freeze in place, or start to crawl?

Switch the color-scheme while the model runs to view the same dynamics through a different palette.


## EXTENDING THE MODEL

Several creatures could be seeded at once instead of just one in the center. A handful of Orbiums placed at different spots and headings would glide around and interact, some pairs passing cleanly through one another while others collide, merge, or destroy each other.

Many more of discovered creatures in Lenia could be added to the creature library, which runs to hundreds; any that are built from a single ring kernel will work without modification.

The neighborhood weighting could reach beyond a single ring. Widening it into several concentric rings, or combining several separate kernels that each drive their own growth response, is what most of the more elaborate creatures depend on, and it would greatly enlarge the variety the model supports.

Each cell could carry several values at once, like interacting color channels, which is how the extended form of Lenia grows multi-colored organisms with richer internal structure.

The bell curve is not the only possible profile. The reference implementation also offers polynomial, rectangular, and stepped shapes for both the neighborhood weighting and the growth response, each giving the world a different character.

A larger grid would give big creatures room to travel without colliding with themselves across the wrap-around edges, and would leave space for several creatures at once.

A running graph of the total amount of life would show at a glance whether a pattern is holding steady, growing, or dying out.


## NETLOGO FEATURES

To keep each step fast, every cell builds its list of neighbors and their weights once during setup and then reuses that list on every step, rather than recomputing it each time.

The wrap-around world lets neighbors be found correctly even across the edges, with no special bookkeeping at the borders.

The DRAW button reads the live mouse position so you can paint interactively, and the color schemes use the palette extension to produce smooth, evenly graded colors.


## RELATED MODELS

The most closely related model in the NetLogo Model's Library is SmoothLife, the model that first made the Game of Life continuous and that directly inspired Lenia. The key difference is how each cell reads its surroundings. SmoothLife measures two separate regions, a small inner disk standing in for the cell itself and an outer ring standing in for its neighbors, and combines those two measurements with a birth-and-death rule. Lenia instead blends the whole neighborhood through a single soft ring into one number, feeds that number to one smooth growth rule, and also lets time advance continuously. That extra smoothness is what gives Lenia its unusually rich variety of stable, freely moving creatures.

The discrete cellular automata in the NetLogo Models Library are relatives too, especially Life. Comparing them with this model shows the step from the original black-and-white, on-or-off rules to their smooth, continuous descendants.


## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Lenia was created by Bert Chan. This model reproduces the Lenia update rule and several of Chan's named creatures; the creatures' starting shapes are taken from the data files in the public Lenia repository.

Repository: https://github.com/Chakazul/Lenia

Project page: https://chakazul.github.io/lenia.html

## HOW TO CITE

If you mention this model or the NetLogo software in a publication, we ask that you include the citations below.

For the model itself:

* Gurkan, C. and Wilensky, U. (2026). NetLogo Lenia model. [link]. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.

Please cite the NetLogo software as:

* Wilensky, U. (1999). NetLogo. http://ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.


]]></info>
